An Operational Amplifier Comparator(or also called voltage comparator) is an open-looped circuit(meaning that the output does not invoke any action in the circuit) that compares an input voltage to a reference voltage and creates a binary output(0 or 1) that tells which of the 2 voltages are higher. Both of these voltages are analog signals, and the reference voltage signal at the other input leg is fixed.

A comparator by itself is a device or circuit that compares the voltage levels of 2 inputs and then chooses either a high or low voltage, and is a basic analog to digital converter(which i'm starting to see is important since it seems alot of inputs in the car are analog signals but many of the components require digital input signals. An example of application could be a temperature sensor. If you want the heater to turn on at below a specific temperature, you can set the comparator reference voltage at the point where you want it to turn on.
